    {
      "id": "zpDL97IJ9biaW_CSSZsp5",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -53273.498474694345,
      "y": -5772.011985334435,
      "width": 1744.478759765625,
      "height": 725,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a6",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1205653353,
      "version": 2646,
      "versionNonce": 115636649,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1784441288903,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "How to handle frequent driver location updates and efficient proximity searches?\n- 10M drivers sending locations every 5s, that's 2M updates/s, either DynamoDB or PostgreSQL will fail, and scaling them becomes expensive.\n- Querying lat/long on wide column DB or relational DB is extremely inefficient\n- 2M writes/s of 100bytes at on demand($1.25 per million WRU) costs $200k/day\n\n\n1. Bad Solution : Use dynamoDB or relational DB\n- Bad choice explained above\n\n\n2. Batch Processing and Specialised Geospatial DB\n- Drivers updates are aggregated over a short interval and then batch processed, reducing no of write operation\n- Use ProtgreSQL's PostGIS plugin which is based on Quad-Trees to perform proximity searches.\nProblems : Batch writing introduces delay leading to poor driver matching and it may not reflect driver's current position.\n\n\n3. Great Solution : Real-Time In-Memory Geospatial Data Store\n- Use Redis and its geospatial data types and commands for storing and querying driver's location.\n- It uses geohashing to encode lat and long into a 52-bit interger score within a sorted set, where each member(eg. driverId) is associated with its geohash score.\n- GEOADD and GEOSEARCH to add and search nearby locations withing a given radius or bounding box. Highly optimized commands and easily handle real-time data\n- Older versions of Redis(< 6.2) used GEORADIUS and GEORADIUSBYMEMBER but are less efficient than GEOSEARCH\n- No batch processing required, as redis can easily handle such volumes and GEOADD overrides previous value so no memory issue.\n- To handle stale data of drivers who go offline, run a periodic cleanup process to erase entries who haven't been updated in last 30s\n- One approach is to maintain a companion sorted set keyed by timestamp, and periodically remove entries older than the threshold from both the timestamp set and the geo set.\n\nProblems : Risk of entire system crash if redis goes down(single point of failure), but could be overcome by :\n    - Redis persistence : enable redis persistence mechanisms like RDB(Redis DB) or AOF(Append only file) to periodically save data to disk.\n    - Redis Sentinel : Use this for high availability, Sentinel provides automatic failover if the master node goes down, ensuring that a replica is promoted to master.\nBut even if we fail, it wouldn't impact us that much as driver location comes in every 5s",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "How to handle frequent driver location updates and efficient proximity searches?\n- 10M drivers sending locations every 5s, that's 2M updates/s, either DynamoDB or PostgreSQL will fail, and scaling them becomes expensive.\n- Querying lat/long on wide column DB or relational DB is extremely inefficient\n- 2M writes/s of 100bytes at on demand($1.25 per million WRU) costs $200k/day\n\n\n1. Bad Solution : Use dynamoDB or relational DB\n- Bad choice explained above\n\n\n2. Batch Processing and Specialised Geospatial DB\n- Drivers updates are aggregated over a short interval and then batch processed, reducing no of write operation\n- Use ProtgreSQL's PostGIS plugin which is based on Quad-Trees to perform proximity searches.\nProblems : Batch writing introduces delay leading to poor driver matching and it may not reflect driver's current position.\n\n\n3. Great Solution : Real-Time In-Memory Geospatial Data Store\n- Use Redis and its geospatial data types and commands for storing and querying driver's location.\n- It uses geohashing to encode lat and long into a 52-bit interger score within a sorted set, where each member(eg. driverId) is associated with its geohash score.\n- GEOADD and GEOSEARCH to add and search nearby locations withing a given radius or bounding box. Highly optimized commands and easily handle real-time data\n- Older versions of Redis(< 6.2) used GEORADIUS and GEORADIUSBYMEMBER but are less efficient than GEOSEARCH\n- No batch processing required, as redis can easily handle such volumes and GEOADD overrides previous value so no memory issue.\n- To handle stale data of drivers who go offline, run a periodic cleanup process to erase entries who haven't been updated in last 30s\n- One approach is to maintain a companion sorted set keyed by timestamp, and periodically remove entries older than the threshold from both the timestamp set and the geo set.\n\nProblems : Risk of entire system crash if redis goes down(single point of failure), but could be overcome by :\n    - Redis persistence : enable redis persistence mechanisms like RDB(Redis DB) or AOF(Append only file) to periodically save data to disk.\n    - Redis Sentinel : Use this for high availability, Sentinel provides automatic failover if the master node goes down, ensuring that a replica is promoted to master.\nBut even if we fail, it wouldn't impact us that much as driver location comes in every 5s",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "xmR6sCK2D9JSmIsa6P-k1",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -53263.10842617552,
      "y": -4944.4223596337,
      "width": 1445.5789794921875,
      "height": 225,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a7",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 703630921,
      "version": 842,
      "versionNonce": 1924021289,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1784441577098,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "How to prevent system overload from frequent driver location update while ensuring high accuracy?\n- 5s update from 10M drivers can easily cause system overload\n\nGreat Solution : Adaptive Location Update Intervals\n- Dynamically adjust the frequency of location updates based on driver's speed, direction of travel, proximity to pending ride req. and driver status.\n- allowing us to reduce location updates while maintaining accuracy.\n- Use mobile's sensors to determine the optimal interval of sending updates eg. if driver is stationary or moving slowly updates can be less frequent\nProblem : Coming up with optimal algo to send frequent updates, requires lots of testing.\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "How to prevent system overload from frequent driver location update while ensuring high accuracy?\n- 5s update from 10M drivers can easily cause system overload\n\nGreat Solution : Adaptive Location Update Intervals\n- Dynamically adjust the frequency of location updates based on driver's speed, direction of travel, proximity to pending ride req. and driver status.\n- allowing us to reduce location updates while maintaining accuracy.\n- Use mobile's sensors to determine the optimal interval of sending updates eg. if driver is stationary or moving slowly updates can be less frequent\nProblem : Coming up with optimal algo to send frequent updates, requires lots of testing.\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "4xVWrQZkdTvhded7VlgY9",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -53255.299213061095,
      "y": -4616.435408828815,
      "width": 1986.37890625,
      "height": 650,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
