      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1784443528788,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "How to prevent multiple ride request sent to same driver?\n- We need consistency for ride matching, meaning we only request one driver at a time for a ride and that each driver receives only one request at a time.\n- The drive would've only 10s to accept/deny the request before we move on and send the ride req. to next driver.\n\n\n1. Bad Solution : App level locking with Manual timeout checks\n- Ride matching service marks a ride request as \"locked\" when its sent to a driver.\n- It then starts a timer for the lock duration. It driver doesn't accepts within this period, the instance manually releases the lock and makes it available to other drivers.\nProblems\n    - lack of coordination : with multiple instances of Ride Matching Service, no centralized coordination, potential race condition, as two instances might attempt to lock same ride request simultaneously.\n    - Inconsistent lock state : if one instance crashed before releasing the lock, other instance have no knowledge of this lock\n    - Can't scale\n\n\n2. Good Solution : DB Status update with Timeout Handling\n- Update the status column in Ride table with \"outstanding_request\" using DB's ACID allowing only one instance to lock\n- If driver accepts update it to \"accepted\" else make it \"available\" again, have simple timeout mechanism within Ride Service to ensure lock is released after 10s if driver doesn't repond\nProblem : Relying on in-memory timeout handling, if ride service crashes, again same problem, one solution is cron job to perform periodic cleanup but introduces additional complexity and delay\n\n\n3. Great Solution : Distributed Lock with TTL\n- Use Redis to create a lock with unique identifier(driverId) with a TTL of 10s, ride matching service attempts to acquire a lock on driverId\n- If successfully acquired, then no other service instance can send a ride request to the same driver until the lock is expired or released.\n- If driver accepts within TTL window, Ride Matching Service update the ride status to \"accepted\" and lock is released in Redis\n- If not accepted within TTL, the lock expires automatically and its available to other riders.\nProblem : single point of failure Redis, have to employ various fail-over strategies to overcome this.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "How to prevent multiple ride request sent to same driver?\n- We need consistency for ride matching, meaning we only request one driver at a time for a ride and that each driver receives only one request at a time.\n- The drive would've only 10s to accept/deny the request before we move on and send the ride req. to next driver.\n\n\n1. Bad Solution : App level locking with Manual timeout checks\n- Ride matching service marks a ride request as \"locked\" when its sent to a driver.\n- It then starts a timer for the lock duration. It driver doesn't accepts within this period, the instance manually releases the lock and makes it available to other drivers.\nProblems\n    - lack of coordination : with multiple instances of Ride Matching Service, no centralized coordination, potential race condition, as two instances might attempt to lock same ride request simultaneously.\n    - Inconsistent lock state : if one instance crashed before releasing the lock, other instance have no knowledge of this lock\n    - Can't scale\n\n\n2. Good Solution : DB Status update with Timeout Handling\n- Update the status column in Ride table with \"outstanding_request\" using DB's ACID allowing only one instance to lock\n- If driver accepts update it to \"accepted\" else make it \"available\" again, have simple timeout mechanism within Ride Service to ensure lock is released after 10s if driver doesn't repond\nProblem : Relying on in-memory timeout handling, if ride service crashes, again same problem, one solution is cron job to perform periodic cleanup but introduces additional complexity and delay\n\n\n3. Great Solution : Distributed Lock with TTL\n- Use Redis to create a lock with unique identifier(driverId) with a TTL of 10s, ride matching service attempts to acquire a lock on driverId\n- If successfully acquired, then no other service instance can send a ride request to the same driver until the lock is expired or released.\n- If driver accepts within TTL window, Ride Matching Service update the ride status to \"accepted\" and lock is released in Redis\n- If not accepted within TTL, the lock expires automatically and its available to other riders.\nProblem : single point of failure Redis, have to employ various fail-over strategies to overcome this.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "mtXpEHYyjLHcIq8ONUaSN",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -53249.442303225325,
      "y": -3874.560162960624,
      "width": 1696.458984375,
      "height": 475,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
