      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 71157511,
      "version": 1851,
      "versionNonce": 1371344967,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1784444756409,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "How to ensure no request are dropped at peak demand period?\n- say after a concert 100k users are requesting rides simultaneously\n\n\n1. Bad Solution : First-Come, First-Serve with No Queue\nProblem : doesn't scale during high demand, if system is overwhelmed it may start dropping requests poor user experience\n- Even if we scale our Ride Matching service, we still might not be able to handle the surge\n- If ride matching service goes down, any ride requests being processed at that instant will be lost.\n\n\n2. Great Solution : Queue with Dynamic Scaling\n- When a ride request comes in add it to the queue, and Ride-Matching then picks the request based on First-Come first serve manner\n- If queue grows large, scale the system horizontally\n- Partition the queue based on geographic regions.\n- Use distributed message queue like Kafka, allowing us to commit the offset of the message queue only after we've successfully found a match.\n- This way if matching service goes down, the ride request will still be in queue and a new instance would pick it up.\nProblem : Managing the queue service is complex, need to use managed queue services like Amazon SQS, Amazon MSK(Managed Streaming for Apache Kafka) or Confluent Cloud\n- Since its FIFO queue, there can be requests that takes a lot of time to match say they are of far away and driver keeps on rejecting them\n- Need to use priority queue instead, allowing us to prioritize request based on proximity, driver rating etc.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "How to ensure no request are dropped at peak demand period?\n- say after a concert 100k users are requesting rides simultaneously\n\n\n1. Bad Solution : First-Come, First-Serve with No Queue\nProblem : doesn't scale during high demand, if system is overwhelmed it may start dropping requests poor user experience\n- Even if we scale our Ride Matching service, we still might not be able to handle the surge\n- If ride matching service goes down, any ride requests being processed at that instant will be lost.\n\n\n2. Great Solution : Queue with Dynamic Scaling\n- When a ride request comes in add it to the queue, and Ride-Matching then picks the request based on First-Come first serve manner\n- If queue grows large, scale the system horizontally\n- Partition the queue based on geographic regions.\n- Use distributed message queue like Kafka, allowing us to commit the offset of the message queue only after we've successfully found a match.\n- This way if matching service goes down, the ride request will still be in queue and a new instance would pick it up.\nProblem : Managing the queue service is complex, need to use managed queue services like Amazon SQS, Amazon MSK(Managed Streaming for Apache Kafka) or Confluent Cloud\n- Since its FIFO queue, there can be requests that takes a lot of time to match say they are of far away and driver keeps on rejecting them\n- Need to use priority queue instead, allowing us to prioritize request based on proximity, driver rating etc.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "p7CoPCOSvrfxi7Szv2afZ",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -53241.900006574804,
      "y": -3284.602329050875,
      "width": 2470.098388671875,
      "height": 525,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aA",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1465618505,
      "version": 1172,
      "versionNonce": 1635968903,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1784445323727,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "What happens if driver fails to respond in a timely manner?\n\n1. Good Solution : Delay Queue\n- If the current driver doesn't respond within the timeout period, so when the ride request is sent to a driver, we simultaneously schedule a delayed message in a queue(Amazon SQL delay queue) that gets processed after the 10s timeout period\n- The delayed message contains the ride request details and the driver who was originally contacted\n- When the delayed message is processed, the system checks if the ride is still unassigned. If so, it automatically moves to the next driver in the ranked list and sends them the ride request, while scheduling another delayed message for the new driver.\nProblems : Managing multiple delay queues and ensuring they are properly cancelled when a driver accepts the ride.\nIf a driver accepts the ride after the delayed message queue is already scheduled but before its processed, need to ensure the delayed queue is handled gracefully and doesn't reassigns the ride.\n\n\n2. Great Solution : Durable Execution\n- Use durable execution framework like Temporal or AWS Step Functions.\n- It provides in-built support for timeouts, retries, state management in way that survives service crashes and restarts.\n- With durable execution, the entire ride matching workflow is modeled as a durable workflow that can handle complex business logic, including driver timeouts, retries, and fallback mechanisms.\n- The workflow maintains its state persistently, so even if the service crashes, the workflow can be resumed from where it left off.\n    - Send the ride request to the first driver\n    - Set a 10-second timeout\n    - If the driver accepts, complete the workflow\n    - If the driver declines or times out, automatically move to the next driver\n    - Continue this process until a driver is found or all drivers are exhausted\nProblem : Additional complexity of workflow orchestration system",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "What happens if driver fails to respond in a timely manner?\n\n1. Good Solution : Delay Queue\n- If the current driver doesn't respond within the timeout period, so when the ride request is sent to a driver, we simultaneously schedule a delayed message in a queue(Amazon SQL delay queue) that gets processed after the 10s timeout period\n- The delayed message contains the ride request details and the driver who was originally contacted\n- When the delayed message is processed, the system checks if the ride is still unassigned. If so, it automatically moves to the next driver in the ranked list and sends them the ride request, while scheduling another delayed message for the new driver.\nProblems : Managing multiple delay queues and ensuring they are properly cancelled when a driver accepts the ride.\nIf a driver accepts the ride after the delayed message queue is already scheduled but before its processed, need to ensure the delayed queue is handled gracefully and doesn't reassigns the ride.\n\n\n2. Great Solution : Durable Execution\n- Use durable execution framework like Temporal or AWS Step Functions.\n- It provides in-built support for timeouts, retries, state management in way that survives service crashes and restarts.\n- With durable execution, the entire ride matching workflow is modeled as a durable workflow that can handle complex business logic, including driver timeouts, retries, and fallback mechanisms.\n- The workflow maintains its state persistently, so even if the service crashes, the workflow can be resumed from where it left off.\n    - Send the ride request to the first driver\n    - Set a 10-second timeout\n    - If the driver accepts, complete the workflow\n    - If the driver declines or times out, automatically move to the next driver\n    - Continue this process until a driver is found or all drivers are exhausted\nProblem : Additional complexity of workflow orchestration system",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "IVTcX43oNKhmnU3oEOCR9",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -53239.68078683234,
      "y": -2638.7521876065057,
      "width": 1159.6591796875,
      "height": 325,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aB",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1991902473,
      "version": 816,
      "versionNonce": 1615133737,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1784445628838,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "How to further scale to reduce latency and improve throughput?\n\n1. Bad Solution : Vertical Scaling\n- add more CPU, memory and storage\nProblem : expensive, requires downtime for server to do that, its not infinitely scalable, not fault tolerant.\n\n\n2. Great Solution : Geo-Sharding with Read Replicas\n- Scale horizontally, add more servers, shard our data geographically, and use read replicas to improve read throughput\n- Reduces latency as distance b/w client and server reduced.\nProblem : ensure Data distributed evenly across shards, use CH for data distribution\nBasically have the exact copy of the current system deployed across various geographies.\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "How to further scale to reduce latency and improve throughput?\n\n1. Bad Solution : Vertical Scaling\n- add more CPU, memory and storage\nProblem : expensive, requires downtime for server to do that, its not infinitely scalable, not fault tolerant.\n\n\n2. Great Solution : Geo-Sharding with Read Replicas\n- Scale horizontally, add more servers, shard our data geographically, and use read replicas to improve read throughput\n- Reduces latency as distance b/w client and server reduced.\nProblem : ensure Data distributed evenly across shards, use CH for data distribution\nBasically have the exact copy of the current system deployed across various geographies.\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
